{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.SchurWeyl.HoefsmitRep"],"declarations":61,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau","kind":"theorem","line":1089,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L1089-L1101","doc":"**The swap negates the axial distance.**  `s_k` exchanges the cells of `k` and `k+1`\n(`cellOf_swapTableau_self` / `_succ`) and `axialDistance` is antisymmetric, so the pair\n`{T, s_k·T}` carries the two distances `d` and `-d`.\n\nThis is what makes the size-`2` block a `seminormalBlock`: its diagonal is\n`(a(d), a(-d))`, whose entries sum to `q - q⁻¹` by `seminormalParam_add_neg`.  Without it\nthe two rows of a pair would be unrelated and the quadratic relation could not close. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":1032,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L1032-L1087","doc":"**The axial distance of a generator is never zero** — consecutive entries never sit on\nthe same diagonal.\n\nIf they did, the cell completing their rectangle would be forced to carry an entry strictly\nbetween `k` and `k+1`.  Concretely, if `k` sits at `(i₁, j₁)` and `k+1` at `(i₂, j₂)` with\nequal contents `j₁ - i₁ = j₂ - i₂`, then the two cells are strictly ordered in *both*\ncoordinates, and the corner cell (`(i₁, j₂)` in one orientation, `(i₂, j₁)` in the other)\nlies in `μ` by up-left closure and is squeezed between them.\n\nThis is what keeps `seminormalParam q (axialDist T k)` away from the pole of\n`(q - q⁻¹)/(1 - q^{-2d})` at `d = 0`, so the parameter is defined at every generator and\nevery tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_trichotomy","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_trichotomy","kind":"theorem","line":1007,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L1007-L1030","doc":"**The orbit trichotomy.**  Exactly one of: the two cells share a row (axial distance\n`+1`), share a column (`-1`), or share neither (`SwapAdmissible`).  This is the case split\nthe generator assembly runs on — the first two give the scalar entries `q` and `-q⁻¹`, the\nthird a `seminormalBlock`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_ne","kind":"theorem","line":997,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L997-L1005","doc":"The cells of two distinct entries are distinct. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_comm","kind":"theorem","line":971,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L971-L984","doc":"**The `s_k`-actions commute at far distance** — `s_k s_l · T = s_l s_k · T` for\n`|k - l| ≥ 2`.  This is the tableau-level form of `σ_k σ_l = σ_l σ_k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_far_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_far_iff","kind":"theorem","line":955,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L955-L969","doc":"**Admissibility at `k` is *unchanged* by a far swap at `l`** — both directions.\n`swapAdmissible_of_far` gives the forward implication; the converse matters just as much,\nbecause a matrix-level argument has to know that a far swap cannot *create* a size-`2`\norbit where there was a scalar. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_of_far","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_of_far","kind":"theorem","line":942,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L942-L953","doc":"**A far swap leaves the axial distance alone.**  `s_l` moves no cell outside\n`{l, l+1}`, so the parameter `σ_k` reads off is unchanged.  Together with\n`swapAdmissible_far_iff` this says a far swap leaves *every* input to `σ_k`'s row rule\nuntouched — which is what a matrix-level far-commutation proof needs. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_adj_words_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_adj_words_ne","kind":"theorem","line":921,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L921-L940","doc":"**`s_{k+1}s_k·T ≠ s_k s_{k+1}·T` — the size-`6` distinguisher.**\n\nThis single inequality is what separates a generic `⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit from a smaller\none: the two length-`2` words land on different tableaux exactly when all six words do.\nRead at the cell holding `k`, the left carries `k+2` and the right carries `k+1`.\n\n(Contrast `swapTableau_comm`, where the *far* generators commute outright.  Here they do\nnot, and that failure is the braid.) ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_adj_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_adj_ne","kind":"theorem","line":909,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L909-L919","doc":"**The two adjacent generators move `T` to different places.**  Read at the cell holding\n`k`: `s_k·T` puts `k+1` there, while `s_{k+1}·T` leaves `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk_sl_sk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk_sl_sk","kind":"theorem","line":893,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L893-L907","doc":"Entries of the longest word `s_k s_{k+1}s_k·T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_braid","kind":"theorem","line":866,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L866-L891","doc":"**The braid relation at the level of tableaux**: the two longest words agree.\n\nThis is what makes the sixth element of a `⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit well defined from either\ndirection, and it is the *undeformed* shadow of the relation the representation must\nsatisfy — at `q = 1` the seminormal matrices degenerate to permutation matrices and the\nHecke braid relation becomes exactly this.\n\nNote the deformed statement does **not** follow from it: the matrix braid needs\n`seminormalParam_rel`, which is invisible here. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_braid","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_braid","kind":"theorem","line":827,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L827-L864","doc":"**The braid relation for value-transpositions.**  `s_k s_{k+1} s_k = s_{k+1} s_k s_{k+1}`\nas permutations of `{k, k+1, k+2}` — the symmetric-group relation the whole construction is\na deformation of.\n\nPerformance note: proved by **pointwise rewriting**, not `unfold swapVal; split_ifs`.  The\nlatter works for the two-swap statements (`swapVal_involutive`, `swapVal_comm`) but the\nthree-swap nesting here has a branch tree that exhausts the default heartbeats — and\nsplitting on `x` first is not enough, because each branch still carries the full nesting.\nRewriting with the three evaluation lemmas keeps every step search-free. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.col_const_of_no_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.col_const_of_no_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":808,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L808-L825","doc":"The mirror: all cells in one column, when the common distance is `-1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.row_const_of_no_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.row_const_of_no_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":788,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L788-L806","doc":"**A tableau no generator moves has all its cells in one row** (when its common axial\ndistance is `+1`).  Chains `sameRow_of_not_admissible_of_one` from `1` up to `j`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameCol_of_not_admissible_of_neg_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameCol_of_not_admissible_of_neg_one","kind":"theorem","line":767,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L767-L786","doc":"The mirror: a fixed generator at distance `-1` fixes it within a column. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameRow_of_not_admissible_of_one","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameRow_of_not_admissible_of_one","kind":"theorem","line":745,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L745-L765","doc":"A fixed generator at distance `+1` fixes it *within a row*: `¬SwapAdmissible` leaves\n\"same row or same column\", and same-column would force distance `-1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_eq_of_no_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_eq_of_no_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":718,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L718-L743","doc":"**A tableau no generator moves has a single axial distance throughout.**\n\nChaining `axialDist_eq_of_not_admissible` along `k, k+1, …`: if every generator fixes `T`,\nthen all of `T`'s axial distances agree — so they are *all* `+1` (every consecutive pair in a\nrow) or *all* `-1` (every pair in a column).\n\nThis is the route to non-scalarity that avoids the full SYT-connectivity theorem.  Rather\nthan showing two tableaux are joined by swaps, it shows that a tableau admitting **no** swap\nat all is forced to be a single row or a single column — and such a shape has only one\ntableau.  So `#SYT μ ≥ 2` must produce a movable tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_word_right_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_word_right_iff","kind":"theorem","line":700,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L700-L716","doc":"**`s_k s_{k+1}·T` is `(k+1)`-admissible exactly when `T` is `k`-admissible.**  The mirror\nof `swapAdmissible_word_left_iff`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_word_left_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_word_left_iff","kind":"theorem","line":682,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L682-L698","doc":"**`s_{k+1}s_k·T` is `k`-admissible exactly when `T` is `(k+1)`-admissible.**  The longer\nword inherits the condition rather than imposing a new one. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_cross_iff","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_cross_iff","kind":"theorem","line":665,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L665-L680","doc":"**`s_k·T` is `(k+1)`-admissible exactly when `s_{k+1}·T` is `k`-admissible.**  Both are\nthe condition on the cells of `k` and `k+2`, so the two \"half-generic\" flag patterns cannot\noccur. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_eq_of_not_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_eq_of_not_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":548,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L548-L642","doc":"**Adjacent generators that both fix `T` fix it with the *same* scalar.**\n\nIf neither `σ_k` nor `σ_{k+1}` moves `T`, then `axialDist T k = axialDist T (k+1)` — both\n`+1` (two cells in a row) or both `-1` (two in a column).  The mixed configurations are\n**impossible**, not merely unobserved.\n\nThat impossibility is what makes the braid relation hold on a size-`1` orbit at all.  There\nboth sides collapse to `a(d_k)²·a(d_l)` and `a(d_l)²·a(d_k)`, whose difference is\n`a(d_k)a(d_l)(a(d_k) - a(d_l))`; with `a(±1) ∈ {q, -q⁻¹}` that vanishes exactly when the two\ndistances agree, and is `-(q + q⁻¹) ≠ 0` when they differ.  So the lemma is not a\nconvenience — without it the braid would be *false* on those orbits.\n\nThe impossibility argument: suppose `k, k+1` share a row and `k+1, k+2` a column.  Adjacency\n(`sameRow_adjacent` / `sameCol_adjacent`) forces the cells to be `(i,j)`, `(i,j+1)`,\n`(i+1,j+1)`, and then the corner `(i+1,j)` lies in `μ` and is squeezed to carry `k+1` — which\n`(i,j+1)` already carries.  The column-then-row case is the mirror. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":544,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L544-L546","doc":"`s_k` fixes everything outside `{k, k+1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_succ","kind":"theorem","line":540,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L540-L542","doc":"`s_k` sends `k+1` back to `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_self","kind":"theorem","line":536,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L536-L538","doc":"`s_k` sends `k` to `k+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk_sl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk_sl","kind":"theorem","line":522,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L522-L534","doc":"Entries of `s_k s_{k+1}·T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sl_sk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sl_sk","kind":"theorem","line":508,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L508-L520","doc":"Entries of `s_{k+1}s_k·T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sl","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sl","kind":"theorem","line":496,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L496-L506","doc":"Entries of `s_{k+1}·T` at the two distinguished cells. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.orbitEntries_sk","kind":"theorem","line":485,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L485-L494","doc":"Entries of `s_k·T` at the two distinguished cells. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_entry_cellOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_entry_cellOf","kind":"theorem","line":451,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L451-L463","doc":"**Reading a swapped tableau at a cell of the original.**  `s_l·T` carries `swapVal l m`\nin the cell where `T` carried `m`.\n\nThis is the primitive that makes the whole `⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit computable: every element\nis a word in the two swaps, so its entry at `cellOf T k` (resp. `cellOf T (k+1)`) is that\nword of `swapVal`s applied to `k` (resp. `k+1`).  Two elements are then distinct as soon as\ntheir words disagree at one of those two values, which `omega` settles after\n`simp only [swapVal]; split_ifs`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.ne_of_entry_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.ne_of_entry_ne","kind":"theorem","line":445,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L445-L449","doc":"Tableaux differing at a single cell are distinct. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_word_right","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_word_right","kind":"theorem","line":427,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L427-L443","doc":"The mirror: `s_k s_{k+1}·T` makes `σ_{k+1}` read `p`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_word_left","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_word_left","kind":"theorem","line":404,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L404-L425","doc":"**After both generators have acted, `σ_k` reads the *other* original distance.**\n`s_{k+1}s_k·T` holds `k` in the cell that held `k+1` and `k+1` in the cell that held `k+2`,\nso `σ_k`'s distance there is `r`, not `p` and not the sum.\n\nWith `axialDist_succ_swapTableau` this completes the parameter bookkeeping for a\n`⟨s_k, s_{k+1}⟩`-orbit: across the six elements only `p`, `r`, `p+r` and their negatives\never occur, which is why the braid closes on the single relation\n`a(p+r)·(a(p) + a(r) - c) = a(p)·a(r)`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_swapTableau_succ","kind":"theorem","line":391,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L391-L402","doc":"The mirror: `s_{k+1}` moves `k+2` into the cell of `k+1` and leaves `k` alone, so\n`σ_k` comes to read the same sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_succ_swapTableau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_succ_swapTableau","kind":"theorem","line":376,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L376-L389","doc":"**An adjacent swap shifts the next generator's distance by the current one.**\n`s_k` moves `k+1` into the cell of `k` and leaves `k+2` alone, so `σ_{k+1}` comes to read\nthe distance from `k`'s cell to `k+2`'s — which is the sum. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_of_far","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_of_far","kind":"theorem","line":351,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L351-L362","doc":"**Admissibility at `k` survives a far swap at `l`**: `s_l` leaves the cells of `k` and\n`k+1` alone, so the \"different row and different column\" condition is untouched. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_of_ne","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_of_ne","kind":"theorem","line":340,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L340-L349","doc":"Applying `s_l` does not move the cell of an entry outside `{l, l+1}`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_comm","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_comm","kind":"theorem","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L335-L338","doc":"Disjoint value-transpositions commute. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_involutive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_involutive","kind":"theorem","line":317,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L317-L326","doc":"**The swap is an involution**: applying `s_k` twice returns `T`.  Together with\n`swapAdmissible_swapTableau` this makes `{T, s_k·T}` a genuine two-element orbit, with no\nside hypothesis about the swapped tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_swapTableau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapAdmissible_swapTableau","kind":"theorem","line":305,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L305-L315","doc":"**Admissibility is symmetric**: if the swap at `k` is admissible for `T` then it is\nadmissible for `s_k·T`.  Derived, not assumed — the swap merely exchanges the two cells,\nso \"different row and different column\" is preserved. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_succ","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_succ","kind":"theorem","line":294,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L294-L303","doc":"The mirror: the cell of `k+1` in `s_k·T` is the cell of `k` in `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_self","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_swapTableau_self","kind":"theorem","line":282,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L282-L292","doc":"**The swap exchanges the cells of `k` and `k+1`** — the cell of `k` in `s_k·T` is the\ncell of `k+1` in `T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_entry","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau_entry","kind":"theorem","line":272,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L272-L274","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapTableau","kind":"def","line":263,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L263-L270","doc":"**`s_k · T` — the swapped tableau, again standard.**  This is the size-`2` orbit\npartner of `T`.  Standardness needs both that the order survives (`swapSsyt`) and that the\nfilling is still a bijection onto `{1,…,|μ|}`, which holds because the value-swap is an\ninvolution of that interval. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.bijOn_swapVal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.bijOn_swapVal","kind":"theorem","line":251,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L251-L261","doc":"The value-swap is a bijection of `{1,…,n}` onto itself when both `k` and `k+1` lie in\nrange — it is an involution mapping the interval into itself. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapSsyt","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapSsyt","kind":"def","line":222,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L222-L249","doc":"`s_k · T` as a semistandard tableau.  Rows stay weakly increasing and columns strictly\nincreasing: the swap can only disturb the order at the two swapped values, and admissibility\nrules out the one configuration that would — `k` and `k+1` in a common row or column. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.not_sameCol_of_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.not_sameCol_of_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":214,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L214-L220","doc":"Under admissibility no column carries both `k` and `k+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.not_sameRow_of_admissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.not_sameRow_of_admissible","kind":"theorem","line":206,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L206-L212","doc":"Under admissibility no row carries both `k` and `k+1`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.SwapAdmissible","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.SwapAdmissible","kind":"def","line":200,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L200-L204","doc":"**Admissibility of the `s_k`-swap**: the cells holding `k` and `k+1` share neither a\nrow nor a column.  By `sameRow_adjacent` / `sameCol_adjacent` this is exactly the\ncomplement of the two size-`1` orbit cases, so it is the size-`2` condition. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapEntry","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapEntry","kind":"def","line":197,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L197-L198","doc":"The entry function of `s_k · T`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_involutive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal_involutive","kind":"theorem","line":194,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L194-L195","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.swapVal","kind":"def","line":191,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L191-L192","doc":"The transposition of the values `k` and `k+1`, as a map `ℕ → ℕ`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_of_sameCol","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_of_sameCol","kind":"theorem","line":166,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L166-L180","doc":"**Same-column orbit**: consecutive entries sharing a column have axial distance `-1`.\nWith `seminormalParam_neg_one` this is the scalar eigenvalue `-q⁻¹`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameCol_adjacent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameCol_adjacent","kind":"theorem","line":146,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L146-L164","doc":"**Consecutive entries in the same column sit in adjacent cells** — the column-strict\nmirror of `sameRow_adjacent`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_of_sameRow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist_of_sameRow","kind":"theorem","line":130,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L130-L144","doc":"**Same-row orbit**: consecutive entries sharing a row have axial distance `+1`.  With\n`seminormalParam_one` this is the scalar eigenvalue `q`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameRow_adjacent","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.sameRow_adjacent","kind":"theorem","line":108,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L108-L128","doc":"**Consecutive entries in the same row sit in adjacent cells.**  A cell strictly between\nthem would have to carry an entry strictly between `k` and `k+1`, and there is no such\nnatural number.  This is the step where standardness — the filling being a bijection onto\nan interval — is used, not merely semistandardness. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.axialDist","kind":"def","line":102,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L102-L106","doc":"**The axial distance of the generator `σ_k`** in the tableau `T`: the content of the\ncell holding `k+1` minus the content of the cell holding `k`.  This is the parameter that\nselects the seminormal block (`seminormalParam`). ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_unique","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_unique","kind":"theorem","line":90,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L90-L100","doc":"**Uniqueness**: any cell carrying entry `k` *is* `cellOf T k`.  This is the inverse\nround trip, and it is what lets one compute `cellOf` from an explicit tableau. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.entry_cellOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.entry_cellOf","kind":"theorem","line":86,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L86-L88","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_mem","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_mem","kind":"theorem","line":83,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L83-L84","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_spec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf_spec","kind":"theorem","line":71,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L71-L81","doc":"For an in-range `k`, `cellOf` really does produce a cell of `μ` carrying entry `k`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.SchurWeyl.TableauCells.cellOf","kind":"def","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/SchurWeyl/TableauCells.lean#L65-L69","doc":"**The cell holding entry `k`.**  The `StandardYoungTableau` field `bijOn` makes the\nentry map a bijection from the cells onto `{1,…,|μ|}`; this is its inverse, made total by\nsending an out-of-range `k` to `(0,0)`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}